     Definition 0.3.3.

    Let A and B be sets. We say “A is a subset of B” if every element of A is also an
    element of B. We denote this A Ď B (or B Ě A). If A is a subset of B and A and B
    are not the same , so that there is some element of B that is not in A then we say
     that A is a proper subset of B. We denote this by A Ă B (or B Ą A).



  Two things to note about subsets:
  • Let A be a set. It is always the case that ∅Ď A.

  • If A is not a subset of B then we write A Ę B. This is the same as saying that there is
    some element of A that is not in B. That is, there is some a P A such that a R B.

 Example 0.3.4 (subsets)

Let S = t1, 2u. What are all the subsets of S? Well — each element of S can either be in
the subset or not (independent of the other elements of the set). So we have 2 ˆ 2 = 4
possibilities: neither 1 nor 2 is in the subset, 1 is but 2 is not, 2 is but 1 is not, and both 1
and 2 are. That is
                             ∅, t1u, t2u, t1, 2u Ď S

This argument can be generalised with a little work to show that a set that contains exactly
n elements has exactly 2n subsets.
