b. In this example we see that  f (b1) is positive. Applying the IVT tells us that there is
some c between a1 and b1 so that f (c) = 0. This new range is a quarter of the length of the
original. If we keep doing this process the range will halve each time until we know that
the zero is inside some tiny range of possible values. This process is called the bisection
method.
