     Definition 2.2.1 (Derivative at a point).

    Let a P R and let f (x) be defined on an open interval9 that contains a.

     • The derivative of f (x) at x = a is denoted f 1(a) and is defined by

                                                                         f a + h ´ f (a)
                                                      f 1(a) = lim
                                    hÑ0       h

              if the limit exists.

     • When the above limit exists, the function  f (x) is said to be differentiable
           at x = a. When the limit does not exist, the function  f (x) is said to be not
          differentiable at x = a.

     • We can equivalently define the derivative f 1(a) by the limit

                                                                            f (x) ´ f (a)
                                                        f 1(a) = lim                      .
                                      xÑa   x ´ a

        To see that these two definitions are the same, we set x = a + h and then
          the limit as h goes to 0 is equivalent to the limit as x goes to a.



