      • While we can compute a Taylor polynomial about any a-value (providing the deriva-
            tives exist), in order to be a useful approximation, we must be able to compute
                  f (a), f 1(a), . . . , f (n)(a) easily. This means we must choose the point a with care. In-
         deed for many functions the choice a = 0 is very natural — hence the prominence of
          Maclaurin polynomials.

      • If we have computed the approximation Tn(x), then we can readily extend this to
          the next Taylor polynomial Tn+1(x) since

                                             1
                          Tn+1(x) = Tn(x) +              f (n+1)(a) ¨ (x ´ a)n+1
                                           (n + 1)!

          This is very useful if we discover that Tn(x) is an insufficient approximation, because
          then we can produce Tn+1(x) without having to start again from scratch.


