     Definition 3.4.25.

    Suppose that you measure, approximately, some quantity. Suppose that the exact
    value of that quantity is Q0 and that your measurement yielded Q0 + ∆Q. Then
    |∆Q| is called the absolute error of the measurement and 100|∆Q| is called the                                                                    Q0
    percentage error of the measurement. As an example, if the exact value is 4 and
    the measured value is 5, then the absolute error is |5 ´ 4| = 1 and the percentage
    error is 100|5´4| = 25. That is, the error, 1, was 25% of the exact value, 4.                    4




