  More generally


     Definition 3.6.3.

    Let  f (x) be a continuous function on the interval [a, b] and suppose its first and
    second derivatives exist on that interval.

     • If f 2(x) ą 0 for all a ă x ă b, then the graph of f lies above its tangent lines
          for a ă x ă b and it is said to be concave up.





     • If f 2(x) ă 0 for all a ă x ă b, then the graph of f lies below its tangent lines
          for a ă x ă b and it is said to be concave down.





     • If f 2(c) = 0 for some a ă c ă b, and the concavity of f changes across x = c,
         then we call (c, f (c)) an inflection point.





