  Now suppose that we want to take the average of a function  f (x) with x running
continuously from a to b. How do we even define what that means? A natural approach
is to

  • select, for each natural number n, a sample of n, more or less uniformly distributed,
     values of x between a and b,

  • take the average of the values of f at the selected points,

  • and then take the limit as n tends to infinity.

Unsurprisingly, this process looks very much like how we computed areas and volumes
previously. So let’s get to it.

