We start by showing that a real number is rational if and only if its decimal expansion
is eventually periodic. We need only consider the expansions of numbers 0 ă x ă 1. If
a number is negative then we can just multiply it by ´1 and not change the expansion.
Similarly if the number is larger than 1 then we can just subtract off the integer part of the
number and leave the expansion unchanged.


