C.1Ĳ Richardson Extrapolation

    There are many approximation procedures in which one first picks a step size h and then
     generates an approximation                                                                              be                            A(h) to some desired quantity A. For example, A might                                     şb                                                                                            b´a     the value of some integral     f (x) dx. For the trapezoidal rule with n steps, ∆x =                                            a                                                             n
    plays the role of the step size. Often the order of the error generated by the procedure is
    known. This means

                                                                                         (E1)               A = A(h) + Khk + K1hk+1 + K2hk+2 +  ¨ ¨ ¨
    with k being some known constant, called the order of the error, and K, K1, K2,                                                                                                şb¨ ¨ ¨ being
    some other (usually unknown) constants. If A(h) is the approximation to  =     f (x) dx                                                                                                                 a                                          A    produced by the trapezoidal rule with ∆x = h, then k = 2. If Simpson’s rule is used, k = 4.
                                                                                        in (E1)        Let’s first suppose that h is small enough that the terms K1hk+1 + K2hk+2 +  ¨ ¨ ¨     are small enough1 that dropping them has essentially no impact. This would give

                      A = A(h) + Khk                                (E2)
    Imagine that we know k, but that we do not know A or K, and think of (E2) as an equation
     that the unknowns A and K have to solve. It may look like we have one equation in the
    two unknowns K, A, but that is not the case. The reason is that (E2) is (essentially) true
     for all (sufficiently small) choices of h. If we pick some h, say h1, and use the algorithm to
    determine A(h1) then (E2), with h replaced by h1, gives one equation in the two unknowns
   A and K, and if we then pick some different h, say h2, and use the algorithm a second time     to determine A(h2) then (E2), with h replaced by h2, gives a second equation in the two
    unknowns A and K. The two equations will then determine both A and K.

