which says that B(h) is an approximation to A whose error is of order k + 1, one better4than A(h)’s.
    If A(h) has been computed for three values of h, we can generate B(h) for two values
of h and repeat the above procedure with a new value of k. And so on. One widely used
numerical integration algorithm, called Romberg integration5, applies this procedure re-
peatedly to the trapezoidal rule. It is known that the trapezoidal rule approximation T(h)
to an integral I has error behaviour (assuming that the integrand f (x) is smooth)

                                   I = T(h) + K1h2 + K2h4 + K3h6 + ¨ ¨ ¨

Only even powers of h appear. Hence

     T(h)                    has error of order 2, so that, using (E6) with k = 2,
