1.2.7 §§ (Optional) Application of Cross Products to Rotational Motion

     In most computations involving rotational motion, the cross product shows up in one
    form or another. This is one of the main applications of the cross product. Consider, for
     example, a rigid body which is rotating at a constant rate of Ωradians per second about
    an axis whose direction is given by the unit vector ˆa. Let P be any point on the body. Let’s
     figure out its velocity. Pick any point on the axis of rotation and designate it as the origin
     of our coordinate system. Denote by r the vector from the origin to the point P. Let θ
     denote the angle between ˆa and r. As time progresses the point P sweeps out a circle of
     radius R = |r | sin θ.



