   So how is this possible? The answer is that fx(0, 0) only involves values of  f (x, y)
with y = 0. As  f (x, 0) = x, for all values of x, we have that  f (x, 0) is a continuous, and
indeed a differentiable, function. Similarly, fy(0, 0) only involves values of  f (x, y) with
x = 0. As f (0, y) = 0, for all values of y, we have that f (0, y) is a continuous, and indeed
a differentiable, function. On the other hand, the bad behaviour of  f (x, y) for (x, y) near
(0, 0) only happens for x and y both nonzero.
