§§§ Moment of Inertia
Consider a plate that fills the region R in the xy-plane, that has mass density ρ(x, y) kg/m2,
and that is rotating at ω rad/s about some axis. Let’s call the axis of rotation A. We arenow going to determine the kinetic energy of that plate. Recall24 that, by definition, the
kinetic energy of a point particle of mass m that is moving with speed v is 12mv2.
   To get the kinetic energy of the entire plate, cut it up into tiny rectangles25, say of size
dx ˆ dy. Think of each rectangle as being (essentially) a point particle. If the point (x, y)
on the plate is a distance D(x, y) from the axis of rotation A, then as the plate rotates, thepoint (x, y) sweeps out a circle of radius D(x, y). The figure on the right below shows
that circle as seen from high up on the axis of rotation.  The circular arc that the point




