1.4Ĳ Curves in Three Dimensions

   So far, we have developed formulae for the curvature, unit tangent vector, etc., at a point
     r(t) on a curve that lies in the xy-plane. We now extend our discussion to curves in R3.
    Fix any t. For t1 very close to t, r(t1), will, by the Taylor expansion to second order, be very
    close to r(t) + r1(t) (t1 ´ t) + 2r2(t)1       (t1 ´ t)2, so that r(t1) almost lies in the plane through     r(t) that is determined by the two vectors r1(t) and r2(t). Thus, if we restrict our attention
    to a very small part of the curve near the point of interest r(t), the curve will, to a very
   good approximation lie in some plane. So we can still define, for example, the osculating
     circle to the curve at r(t) to be the circle in that plane that fits the curve best near r(t). And
   we still have the formulae16

