2.5.1  The Deﬁnition of Linear Independence

Essential Deﬁnition. A set of vectors {v1, v2,..., vk} is linearly independent if
the vector equation
                          x1v1 + x2v2 + ··· + xkvk = 0
has only the trivial solution x1 = x2 = ··· = xk = 0. The set {v1, v2,..., vk} is
linearly dependent otherwise.
   In other words, {v1, v2,..., vk} is linearly dependent if there exist numbers
x1, x2,..., xk, not all equal to zero, such that
                          x1v1 + x2v2 + ··· + xkvk = 0.

This is called a linear dependence relation or equation of linear dependence.

  Note that linear dependence and linear independence are notions that apply
  to a collection of vectors.  It does not make sense to say things like “this vec-
  tor is linearly dependent on these other vectors,” or “this matrix is linearly
  independent.”


Example (Checking linear dependence). Is the set

                                                1     1     3
                                                  1    −1    1                 ,   ,  
               1     2     4 



2.5. LINEAR INDEPENDENCE                                      67


linearly independent?

Solution.  Equivalently, we are asking if the homogeneous vector equation

                                             1       1       3     0
                     x  1    y  −1    z  1     0              +  +  =  
                     1       2       4     0

has a nontrivial solution. We solve this by forming a matrix and row reducing (we
do not augment because of this observation in Section 2.4):

                                               1  1  3               1  0  2
                  1 −1  1    −−−−−→row reduce    0  1  1                             
                  1  2  4               0  0  0

This says x = −2z and y = −z. So there exist nontrivial solutions: for instance,
taking z = 1 gives this equation of linear dependence:

                                              1      1      3     0
                −2  1    −1     1     0               −  +  =  .
                       1      2      4     0



