Writing a subspace as a column space or a null space  A subspace can be given
to you in many different forms. In practice, computations involving subspaces are
much easier if your subspace is the column space or null space of a matrix. The
simplest example of such a computation is ﬁnding a spanning set: a column space
is by deﬁnition the span of the columns of a matrix, and we showed above how
to compute a spanning set for a null space using parametric vector form. For this
reason, it is useful to rewrite a subspace as a column space or a null space before
trying to answer questions about it.


