Proof. First suppose that the reduced row echelon form of (A | In ) does not have
the form ( In | B ). This means that fewer than n pivots are contained in the ﬁrst n
columns (the non-augmented part), so A has fewer than n pivots. It follows that
Nul(A) ̸= {0} (the equation Ax = 0 has a free variable), so there exists a nonzero
vector v in Nul(A). Suppose that there were a matrix B such that BA = In. Then

                           v = Inv = BAv = B0 = 0,
which is impossible as v ̸= 0. Therefore, A is not invertible.
  Now suppose that the reduced row echelon form of (A  | In ) has the form
