3.5.3  Solving Linear Systems using Inverses

In this subsection, we learn to solve Ax = b by “dividing by A.”

Theorem. Let A be an invertible n × n matrix, and let b be a vector in Rn. Then the
matrix equation Ax = b has exactly one solution:

                                 x = A−1b.

Proof. We calculate:

                   Ax = b  =⇒   A−1(Ax) = A−1b
                  =⇒   (A−1A)x = A−1b
                  =⇒   Inx = A−1b
                  =⇒   x = A−1b.

Here we used associativity of matrix multiplication, and the fact that Inx = x for
any vector b.

