                                          2
In other words, dividing by 2 undoes the transformation that multiplies by 2.
   Deﬁne  f : R →R by  f (x) = x3.  This is an invertible transformation, with
inverse g(x) = 3px. Indeed,

                                                                  3                             f ◦g(x) = f (g(x)) = f (3px) = 3px = x

and
                                            3p                   g ◦f (x) = g(f (x)) = g(x3) =   x3 = x.

In other words, taking the cube root undoes the transformation that takes a num-
ber to its cube.
   Deﬁne f : R →R by f (x) = x2. This is not an invertible function. Indeed, we
have f (2) = 2 = f (−2), so there is no way to undo f : the inverse transformation
would not know if it should send 2 to 2 or −2. More formally, if g : R →R satisﬁes
g(f (x)) = x, then

           2 = g(f (2)) = g(2)  and  −2 = g(f (−2)) = g(2),

which is impossible: g(2) is a number, so it cannot be equal to 2 and −2 at the
same time.
   Deﬁne  f : R →R by  f (x) = ex. This is not an invertible function. Indeed, if
there were a function g : R →R such that f ◦g = IdR, then we would have
               −1 = f ◦g(−1) = f (g(−1)) = eg(−1).

But ex is a positive number for every x, so this is impossible.



