                                  0  0  0  0
   0  5  10      3  −9   2               3π  3e  33         ,          ,                                          .                       4  2  5  12 ,   0 −7  3       2  −6  16                        12 −7  2
                             −1  3  4  8

   The proofs of the multiplicativity property and the transpose property below,
as well as the cofactor expansion theorem in Section 4.2 and the determinants
and volumes theorem in Section 4.3, use the following strategy: deﬁne another
function d : {n × n matrices} →R, and prove that d satisﬁes the same four deﬁning
properties as the determinant. By the existence theorem, the function d is equal to
the determinant. This is an advantage of deﬁning a function via its properties: in
order to prove it is equal to another function, one only has to check the deﬁning
properties.



