Theorem (Eigenvalues are roots of the characteristic polynomial). Let A be an n×n
matrix, and let f (λ) = det(A−λIn) be its characteristic polynomial. Then a number
λ0 is an eigenvalue of A if and only if f (λ0) = 0.
Proof. By the invertible matrix theorem in Section 5.1, the matrix equation (A −
λ0In)x = 0 has a nontrivial solution if and only if det(A−λ0In) = 0. Therefore,
    λ0 is an eigenvalue of A ⇐⇒Ax = λ0x has a nontrivial solution
                   ⇐⇒(A−λ0In)x = 0 has a nontrivial solution
                 ⇐⇒A−λ0In is not invertible
                     ⇐⇒det(A−λ0In) = 0
              ⇐⇒f (λ0) = 0.



