   In this section, we study in detail the situation when two matrices behave sim-
ilarly with respect to different coordinate systems. In Section 5.4 and Section 5.5,
we will show how to use eigenvalues and eigenvectors to ﬁnd a simpler matrix
that behaves like a given matrix.


5.3.1  Similar Matrices

We begin with the algebraic deﬁnition of similarity.

Deﬁnition. Two n × n matrices A and B are similar if there exists an invertible
n × n matrix C such that A = CBC−1.

Example. The matrices

                −12  15        3  0 
                                and                   −10  13           0 −2

are similar because
