  Recipe: Computing Ax in terms of B. Suppose that A = CBC−1, where C is
  an invertible matrix with columns v1, v2,..., vn. Let B = {v1, v2,..., vn}, a basis
  for Rn. Let x be a vector in Rn. To compute Ax, one does the following:

     1. Multiply x by C−1, which changes to the B-coordinates: [x]B = C−1x.
     2. Multiply this by B: B[x]B = BC−1x.

     3. Interpreting this vector as a B-coordinate vector, we multiply it by C to
       change back to the usual coordinates: Ax = CBC−1x = CB[x]B.



