5.4  Expectation and Moment Generating Functions

5.4.1  The Expectation Operator

We next generalize some of the concepts from Section 5.1.2. There we saw that every1 PMF has two important
numbers associated with it:

            X     X                         µ =    x fX(x),  σ2 =    (x −µ)2 fX(x).                           (5.12)
                                            x∈S                    x∈S

    Intuitively, for repeated observations of X we would expect the sample mean to closely approximate µ
as the sample size increases without bound. For this reason we call µ the expected value of X and we write
µ = EX, where E is an expectation operator.

Deﬁnition 5.10 (Expected value.). More generally, given a function g we deﬁne the expected value of g(X)
by
                            E g(X) = X g(x) fX(x),                                    (5.13)
                                                            x∈S
provided the (potentially inﬁnite) series P x |g(x)|f(x) is convergent. We say that Eg(X) exists.

    In this notation the variance is σ2 = E(X −µ)2 and we prove the identity

                               E(X −µ)2 = EX2 −(EX)2                                   (5.14)

in Exercise 18.  Intuitively, for repeated observations of X we would expect the sample mean of the g(X)
values to closely approximate E g(X) as the sample size increases without bound.
   Let us take the analogy further. If we expect g(X) to be close to Eg(X) on the average, where would we
expect 3g(X) to be on the average? It could only be 3Eg(X). The following theorem makes this idea precise.

Proposition 5.11. For any functions g and h, any random variable X, and any constant c:

   1. E c = c,

   2. E[c · g(X)] = cEg(X)

   3. E[g(X) + h(X)] = Eg(X) + Eh(X), provided Eg(X) and Eh(X) exist.

Proof. Go directly from the deﬁnition. For example,

                     E[c · g(X)] = X c · g(x)fX(x) = c · X g(x) fX(x) = cEg(X).
                                            x∈S                      x∈S

