                                                   t→∞ t2
                                   = 3,

which closes the example with σ2 = 3 −(3/2)2 = 3/4.


How to do it with R

There exist utilities to calculate probabilities and expectations for general continuous random variables, but
it is better to ﬁnd a built-in model, if possible. Sometimes it is not possible. We show how to do it the long
way, and the distr package [179] way.

Example 6.5. Let X have PDF f(x) = 3x2, 0 < x < 1 and ﬁnd P(0.14 ≤X ≤0.71). (We will ignore that X is
a beta random variable for the sake of argument.)

> f <- function(x) 3*x^2
> integrate(f, lower = 0.14, upper = 0.71)

0.355167 with absolute error < 3.9e-15

   Compare this to the answer we found in Example 6.3. We could integrate the function x· f(x) = 3x3 from
zero to one to get the mean, and use the shortcut σ2 = EX2 −(EX)2 for the variance.

Example 6.6. Let X have PDF f(x) = 3/x4, x > 1. We may integrate the function g(x) = x · f(x) = 3/x3
from zero to inﬁnity to get the mean of X.

> g <- function(x) 3/x^3
> integrate(g, lower = 1, upper = Inf)

1.5 with absolute error < 1.7e-14

   Compare this to the answer we got in Example 6.4. Use -Inf for −∞.

Example 6.7. Let us redo Example 6.3 with the distr package.

   The method is similar to that encountered in Section 5.1.2 in Chapter 5. We deﬁne an absolutely contin-
uous random variable:

> f <- function(x) 3*x^2
> X <- AbscontDistribution(d = f, low1 = 0, up1 = 1)
> p(X)(0.71) - p(X)(0.14)

[1] 0.355167

   Compare this to the answer we found earlier. Now let us try expectation with the distrEx package [178]:

> #E(X); var(X); 3/80

   Compare these answers to the ones we found in Example 6.3. Why are they diﬀerent? Because the
distrEx package resorts to numerical methods when it encounters a model it does not recognize.  This
means that the answers we get for calculations may not exactly match the theoretical values. Be careful.



