6.4  Functions of Continuous Random Variables

The goal of this section is to determine the distribution of U = g(X) based on the distribution of X. In the
discrete case all we needed to do was back substitute for x = g−1(u) in the PMF of X (sometimes accumulating
probability mass along the way). In the continuous case, however, we need more sophisticated tools. Now
would be a good time to review Appendix E.2.

6.4.1  The PDF Method

Proposition 6.20. Let X have PDF fX and let g be a function which is one-to-one with a diﬀerentiable inverse
g−1. Then the PDF of U = g(X) is given by

                                                d
