                                                du
Example 6.22. Let X ∼norm(mean = µ, sd = σ), and let Y = eX. What is the PDF of Y? Solution: Notice
ﬁrst that ex > 0 for any x, so the support of Y is (0, ∞). Since the transformation is monotone, we can solve
y = ex for x to get x = ln y, giving dx/dy = 1/y. Therefore, for any y > 0,

                                     1     1       ((ln y −µ)2 )  1
                              fY(y) = fX(ln y) ·   =  √   exp                       ·                                          y  σ  2π       2σ2       y,

where we have dropped the absolute value bars since y > 0. The random variable Y is said to have a lognormal
distribution; see Section 6.5.

Example 6.23. Suppose X ∼norm(mean = 0, sd = 1) and let Y = 4 −3X. What is the PDF of Y?

   The support of X is (−∞, ∞), and as x goes from −∞to ∞, the quantity y = 4−3x also traverses (−∞, ∞).
