                                    36    36  36

   Independent random variables are very useful to the mathematician. They have many, many, tractable
properties. We mention some of the more important ones.

Proposition 7.12.  If X and Y are independent, then for any functions u and v,

                         E (u(X)v(Y)) = (Eu(X)) (Ev(Y)) .                                (7.25)

Proof. This is straightforward from the deﬁnition.
               "
                   E (u(X)v(Y))  =        u(x)v(y) fX,Y(x, y) dxdy
               "
                           =        u(x)v(y) fX(x) fY(y) dxdy

                              Z          Z
                           =     u(x) fX(x) dx    v(y) fY(y) dy

and this last quantity is exactly (Eu(X)) (Ev(Y)).                                     □


  Now that we have Proposition 7.12 we mention a corollary that will help us later to quickly identify those
random variables which are not independent.

Corollary 7.13.  If X and Y are independent, then Cov(X, Y) = 0, and consequently, Corr(X, Y) = 0.

Proof. When X and Y are independent then EXY = EX EY. And when the covariance is zero the numerator
of the correlation is 0.                                                     □


Remark 7.14. Unfortunately, the converse of Corollary 7.13 is not true. That is, there are many random
variables which are dependent yet their covariance and correlation is zero.

   For more details, see Casella and Berger [30]. Proposition 7.12 is useful to us and we will receive mileage
out of it, but there is another fact which will play an even more important role. Unfortunately, the proof is
beyond the techniques presented here. The inquisitive reader should consult Casella and Berger [30], Resnick
[173], etc.

Fact 7.15.  If X and Y are independent, then u(X) and v(Y) are independent for any functions u and v.



