7.4.2  Combining Independent Random Variables

Another important corollary of Proposition 7.12 will allow us to ﬁnd the distribution of sums of random
variables.

Corollary 7.16.  If X and Y are independent, then the moment generating function of X + Y is

                                    MX+Y(t) = MX(t) · MY(t).                                   (7.26)

Proof. Choose u(x) = ex and v(y) = ey in Proposition 7.12, and remember the identity et(x+y) = etx ety.   □

   Let us take a look at some examples of the corollary in action.

Example 7.17. Let X ∼binom(size = n1, prob = p) and Y ∼binom(size = n2, prob = p) be indepen-
dent. Then X + Y has MGF

                                                                            n1             n2              n1+n2
                   MX+Y(t) = MX(t) MY(t) = q + pet   q + pet  = q + pet          ,

which is the MGF of a binom(size = n1 + n2, prob = p) distribution. Therefore, X + Y ∼binom(size =
n1 + n2, prob = p).

Example 7.18. Let X ∼norm(mean = µ1, sd = σ1) and Y ∼norm(mean = µ2, sd = σ2) be independent.
Then X + Y has MGF

                       MX(t) MY(t)  =  exp nµ1t + t2σ21/2 o exp nµ2t + t2σ22/2o
                          =  exp n(µ1 + µ2) t + t2 σ21 + σ22 /2o ,

                          q
which is the MGF of a norm mean = µ1 + µ2, sd =  σ21 + σ22  distribution.

   Even when we cannot use the MGF trick to identify the exact distribution of a linear combination of
random variables, we can still say something about its mean and variance.

Proposition 7.19. Let X1 and X2 be independent with respective population means µ1 and µ2 and population
standard deviations σ1 and σ2. For given constants a1 and a2, deﬁne Y = a1X1 + a2X2. Then the mean and
standard deviation of Y are given by the formulas

                                                                                       1/2
                           µY = a1µ1 + a2µ2,  σY = a21σ21 + a22σ22       .                          (7.27)

Proof. We use Proposition 5.11 to see

                  EY = E (a1X1 + a2X2) = a1EX1 + a2EX2 = a1µ1 + a2µ2.

For the standard deviation, we will ﬁnd the variance and take the square root at the end. And to calculate the
variance we will ﬁrst compute EY2 with an eye toward using the identity σ2Y = EY2 −(EY)2 as a ﬁnal step.

                   EY2 = E (a1X1 + a2X2)2 = E a21X21 + a22X22 + 2a1a2X1X2  .

Using linearity of expectation the E distributes through the sum. Now EX2i = σ2i + µ2i , for i = 1 and 2 and
EX1X2 = EX1EX2 = µ1µ2 because of independence. Thus

                     EY2  =  a21(σ21 + µ21) + a22(σ22 + µ22) + 2a1a2µ1µ2,
                     =  a21σ21 + a22σ22 + a21µ21 + a22µ22 + 2a1a2µ1µ2  .

But notice that the expression in the parentheses is exactly (a1µ1 + a2µ2)2 = (EY)2, so the proof is complete.
