How to do it with R

It is possible to do the computations above in R with the Ryacas package. The package is an interface to
the open-source computer algebra system, “Yacas”. The user installs Yacas, then employs Ryacas to submit
commands to Yacas, after which the output is displayed in the R console.
   There are not yet any examples of Yacas in this book, but there are online materials to help the interested
reader: see http://r-cas.github.io/ryacas/ to get started.

7.8  Remarks for the Multivariate Case

There is nothing spooky about n ≥3 random variables. We just have a whole bunch of them: X1, X2,..., Xn,
which we can shorten to X = (X1, X2, . . . , Xn)T to make the formulas prettier (now may be a good time to
check out Appendix E.5). For X supported on the set S X, the joint PDF fX (if it exists) satisﬁes

                                          fX(x) > 0,   for x ∈S X,                                    (7.43)

and
                     ZZ   Z
                                                                  · · ·    fX(x) dx1dx2 · · · dxn = 1,                              (7.44)

or even shorter: R fX(x) dx = 1. The joint CDF FX is deﬁned by

                            FX(x) = P(X1 ≤x1, X2 ≤x2, . . . , Xn ≤xn),                          (7.45)

for x ∈Rn. The expectation of a function g(X) is deﬁned just as we would imagine:

                                Z
                                 Eg(X) =    g(x) fX(x) dx.                                   (7.46)


provided the integral exists and is ﬁnite. And the moment generating function in the multivariate case is
deﬁned by

                                   MX(t)  =  E exp ntTXo ,                                   (7.47)

whenever the integral exists and is ﬁnite for all t in a neighborhood of 0n×1 (note that tTX is shorthand for
t1X1 + t2X2 + · · · + tnXn). The only diﬀerence in any of the above for the discrete case is that integrals are
replaced by sums.



