Any random variable X with the preceding PDF is said to have Student’s t distribution with r degrees of
freedom, and we write X ∼t(df = r). The shape of the PDF is similar to the normal, but the tails are
considerably heavier. See Figure 8.1. As with the normal distribution, there are four functions in R associated
with the t distribution, namely dt, pt,qt, and rt, which compute the PDF, CDF, quantile function, and
generate random variates, respectively.
