8.5  Simulated Sampling Distributions

There are many, many useful statistics besides sample means, variances, and diﬀerences/ratios of the two.
We would like to ﬁnd their sampling distributions, too, but unless we are lucky, the answer will be not quite
so tidy to write down in a closed-form expression. What do we do then?
   As it turns out, we do not need to know the exact analytical form of the sampling distribution; sometimes
it is enough to approximate it with a simulated distribution. In this section we will show you how. Note that
R is particularly well suited to compute simulated sampling distributions, much more so than, say, SPSS or
SAS.

8.5.1  The Interquartile Range

We use the IQR all of the time; it is one of our go-to measures of spread when our data are skewed or show
outliers. What is the sampling distribution of the IQR?  It turns out that there is a lot we can say about the
IQR, and there are many texts that do, see [?] for one. But as with many results involving sample quantiles
and the order statistics, the formulas are messy and often we are limited to large-sample results (which are
almost all asymptotically normal).
   Never fear. Suppose we would like to know the sampling distribution of the IQR when sampling from,
say, a norm(mean = 0, sd = 1), distribution, for samples of size n = 15. No problem. Let us use the computer
to generate 1000 samples of size 15 from a norm(mean = 0, sd = 1) distribution. For each generated sample,
we will compute an IQR. We will then have 10,000 IQR’s to play with, and all the methods of Chapter ?? at
our disposal to describe them.
   Perhaps the quickest way to do what we want with R is via the replicate function. Here we will
generate all the samples and calculate all the IQR’s in one fell swoop:

> iqrs <- replicate(10000, IQR(rnorm(15)))

  We can look at the mean of the simulated values

> mean(iqrs)    # close to 1.23

[1] 1.231971

   and we can see the standard deviation

> sd(iqrs) # close to 0.35

[1] 0.3563445

  Now let’s take a look at a plot of the simulated values.

> hist(iqrs, breaks = 20)


8.5.2  The Median Absolute Deviation

The MAD is another statistic that we know well, and which also has an intractable sampling distribution. Let
us investigate its sampling distribution for samples of size n = 17 from an exp(rate = 1) population. We
will again simulate 10,000 MAD’s.

> mads <- replicate(10000, mad(rexp(17)))

  We can look at the mean and standard deviation of the simulated values:

> mean(mads); sd(mads)

[1] 0.6812777

[1] 0.2503567

  Now let’s take a look at a plot of the simulated values



