                                             n
                                    1
                          =    (EX1 + EX2 + · · · + EXn) ,
                                    n
                                    1
                          =    (np) ,
                                    n
                          =   p,

which is exactly the same as the parameter which we estimated. More concisely, E ˆp = p, that is, on the
average, the estimator is exactly right.

Deﬁnition 9.7 (Unbiased estimators.). Let s(X1, X2, . . . , Xn) be a statistic which estimates θ. If

                                      Es(X1, X2, . . . , Xn) = θ,

then the statistic s(X1, X2, . . . , Xn) is said to be an unbiased estimator of θ. Otherwise, it is biased.

Example 9.8. Let X1, X2, ..., Xn be an S RS (n) from a norm(mean = µ, sd = σ) distribution.  It can be
shown (in Exercise 34) that if θ = (µ, σ2) then the MLE of θ is

                                                                                        ˆθ = (ˆµ, ˆσ2),                                              (9.3)

where ˆµ = X and
