See Section 12.6 for a better way to write these. The signs of the DFBETAS indicate whether the coeﬃcients
would increase or decrease as a result of including the observation.  If the DFBETAS are large, then the
observation has a large impact on those regression coeﬃcients. We label observations as suspicious if their
DFBETAS have magnitude greater 1 for small data or 2/ √n for large data sets. We can calculate the
DFBETAS with the dfbetas function (some output has been omitted):

> dfb <- dfbetas(cars.lm)
> head(dfb)

  (Intercept)       speed
1  0.09440188 -0.08624563
2  0.29242487 -0.26715961
3 -0.10749794  0.09369281
