  We see that the inclusion of the ﬁrst observation slightly increases the Intercept and slightly decreases
the coeﬃcient on speed.
  We can measure the inﬂuence that an observation has on its ﬁtted value with DFFITS . These are cal-
culated by deleting an observation, reﬁtting the model, recalculating the ﬁt, then standardizing. The formula
is
                                                                              ˆYi −ˆY(i)
                       (DFFITS )i =               ,    i = 1, 2, . . . , n.                         (11.66)                                       S (i) √hii
The value represents the number of standard deviations of ˆYi that the ﬁtted value ˆYi increases or decreases
with the inclusion of the ith observation. We can compute them with the dffits function.

> dff <- dffits(cars.lm)
> dff[1:5]

          1           2           3           4
 0.09490289  0.29397684 -0.11039550  0.22487854
          5
 0.03553887

  A rule of thumb is to ﬂag observations whose DFFIT exceeds one in absolute value, but there are none
of those in this data set.


Cook’s Distance

The DFFITS are good for measuring the inﬂuence on a single ﬁtted value, but we may want to measure the
inﬂuence an observation has on all of the ﬁtted values simultaneously. The statistics used for measuring this
are Cook’s distances which may be calculated4 by the formula

                                 E2i             hii
                            Di =           2  ·                ,    i = 1, 2, . . . , n.                       (11.67)                                 (p + 1)S    (1 −hii)2

It shows that Cook’s distance depends both on the residual Ei and the leverage hii and in this way Di contains
information about outlying x and y values.
   To assess the signiﬁcance of D, we compare to quantiles of an f(df1 = 2, df2 = n −2) distribution. A
rule of thumb is to classify observations falling higher than the 50th percentile as being extreme.

   4Cook’s distances are actually deﬁned by a diﬀerent formula than the one shown. The formula in Equation (11.67) is algebraically
equivalent to the deﬁning formula and is, in the author’s opinion, more transparent.



