                                              −1                                                        −1
We showed in Section 12.2.1 that b = XTX   XTY, which is really just a big matrix – namely XTX  XT
– multiplied by Y. It stands to reason that the sampling distribution of b would be intimately related to the
distribution of Y, which we assumed to be

                  Y ∼mvnorm mean = Xβ, sigma = σ2I  .                        (12.27)

Now recall Theorem 7.33 that we said we were going to need eventually (the time is now). That proposition
guarantees that
                                                                           −1
                     b ∼mvnorm mean = β, sigma = σ2 XTX       ,                      (12.28)

since
                           Eb = XTX −1 XT(Xβ) = β,                               (12.29)

and
                                            −1                  −1             −1
                         Var(b) = XTX   XT(σ2I)X XTX  = σ2 XTX     ,                   (12.30)

                                                     −1
the ﬁrst equality following because the matrix XTX    is symmetric.
   There is a lot that we can glean from Equation (12.28). First, it follows that the estimator b is unbiased
(see Section 9.1). Second, the variances of b0, b1, ..., bn are exactly the diagonal elements of σ2 XTX −1,
which is completely known except for that pesky parameter σ2. Third, we can estimate the standard error of bi
(denoted S bi) with the mean square error S (deﬁned in the previous section) multiplied by the corresponding
diagonal element of XTX −1. Finally, given estimates of the standard errors we may construct conﬁdence
intervals for βi with an interval that looks like

                                              bi ± tα/2(df = n −p −1)S bi.                               (12.31)

The degrees of freedom for the Student’s t distribution2 are the same as the denominator of S 2.

