12.2.5  Conﬁdence and Prediction Intervals

We saw in Section 12.2.2 how to make point estimates of the mean value of additional observations and
predict values of future observations, but how good are our estimates? We need conﬁdence and prediction
intervals to gauge their accuracy, and lucky for us the formulas look similar to the ones we saw in SLR.
    In Equation (12.21) we wrote ˆY(x0) = xT0b, and in Equation (12.28) we saw that

                                                                           −1
                     b ∼mvnorm mean = β, sigma = σ2 XTX       .                      (12.32)


The following is therefore immediate from Theorem 7.33:

                                                                              −1
                          ˆY(x0) ∼mvnorm mean = xT0β, sigma = σ2xT0 XTX   x0  .                (12.33)

It should be no surprise that conﬁdence intervals for the mean value of a future observation at the location
x0 = h x10   x20    . . .   xp0 iT are given by

                              q         −1
                                 ˆY(x0) ± tα/2(df = n −p −1) S  xT0 XTX    x0.                      (12.34)

                     −1
Intuitively, xT0 XTX   x0 measures the distance of x0 from the center of the data. The degrees of freedom in
the Student’s t critical value are n −(p + 1) because we need to estimate p + 1 parameters.
    Prediction intervals for a new observation at x0 are given by

                             q             −1
                               ˆY(x0) ± tα/2(df = n −p −1) S  1 + xT0 XTX    x0.                    (12.35)

The prediction intervals are wider than the conﬁdence intervals, just as in Section 11.2.5.


