> binom.confint(2, 10, methods = "wilson")

  method x  n mean      lower     upper
1 wilson 2 10  0.2 0.05668215 0.5098375

This is the conﬁdence interval reported from the prop.test function when correct = FALSE (see below).
The Wilson interval outperforms the Wald interval, but has poor coverage (less than 1 −α) when π is near 0
or 1.

15.1.3  Agresti-Coull interval (1998)

Let ˜y = y + 0.5z2α/2 and ˜n = n + z2α/2, and set ˜π = ˜y/˜n. The conﬁdence interval is

                      r ˜π(1 −˜π)
                                                                   ˜π ± zα/2                   .                                      (15.3)
                                               n

> binom.confint(2, 10, methods = "ac")

         method x  n mean      lower     upper
1 agresti-coull 2 10  0.2 0.04588727 0.5206324

For a 95% interval (so zα/2 ≈2), this is the famous “add two successes and two failures” rule of thumb that
yields
                                                                           ˜π ≈y + 2                                            (15.4)                                           n + 4.
This interval performs much better than the Wald interval, and contains the Wilson interval.  It does better
than the Wilson interval when π is near 0 or 1.

15.1.4  Clopper-Pearson interval (1934)

The formula for this interval is diﬃcult to write down compactly, but it comes from inverting the exact
binomial test which is based on the binomial distribution.

> binom.confint(2, 10, methods = "exact")

  method x  n mean      lower     upper
1  exact 2 10  0.2 0.02521073 0.5560955

This interval always has coverage ≥1 −α, but it is often too conservative.

15.1.5  Comparison

There are actually 11 diﬀerent conﬁdence intervals for a population proportion supported in package binom,
and they all have their advantages and disadvantages. Below are all 11 intervals for an observed y = 2
successes in n = 10 independent trials.

> binom.confint(2, 10, methods = "all")



15.2. TWO-WAY CONTINGENCY TABLES                                              307


          method x  n      mean       lower     upper
1  agresti-coull 2 10 0.2000000  0.04588727 0.5206324
2     asymptotic 2 10 0.2000000 -0.04791801 0.4479180
3          bayes 2 10 0.2272727  0.02346550 0.4618984
4        cloglog 2 10 0.2000000  0.03090902 0.4747147
5          exact 2 10 0.2000000  0.02521073 0.5560955
6          logit 2 10 0.2000000  0.05041281 0.5407080
7         probit 2 10 0.2000000  0.04206918 0.5175162
8        profile 2 10 0.2000000  0.03711199 0.4994288
9            lrt 2 10 0.2000000  0.03636544 0.4994445
10     prop.test 2 10 0.2000000  0.03542694 0.5578186
11        wilson 2 10 0.2000000  0.05668215 0.5098375


15.1.6  Hypothesis tests

Due to the deep connection between conﬁdence intervals and hypothesis tests (see Remark 10.11), we can
always construct a conﬁdence interval and see if it covers a null hypothesized value. So that means the above
11 intervals correspond to 11 diﬀerent hypothesis tests we now have at our disposal. But the conﬁdence
interval method does not generate a p-value. Neverthelesss, the prop.test function is also known as the
“Score Test” and will generate a p-value.

> prop.test(x = 2, n = 10, p = 0.5, correct = FALSE)

        1-sample proportions test without continuity
        correction

data:  2 out of 10, null probability 0.5
X-squared = 3.6, df = 1, p-value = 0.05778
alternative hypothesis: true p is not equal to 0.5
95 percent confidence interval:
 0.05668215 0.50983753
sample estimates:
  p
0.2

Notice that the conﬁdence interval reported by prop.test when correct = FALSE matches the wilson
conﬁdence interval reported above.


15.2  Two-way Contingency Tables

A two-way table looks like this:

                Rating
School           Average Outstanding Poor
  Adequate             8          14    2
  Good                25           3   36
  Most desirable      20          21    4
  Undesirable          7          10    6

   There are two variables (hence the name): a row variable and a column variable. The numbers in the body
of the table are counts of observations falling into the respective row/column combinations.



308                               CHAPTER 15. CATEGORICAL DATA ANALYSIS

15.2.1  Enter by hand

If you already have a two-way table (say, from a textbook or website) then you can enter it into the computer
manually like this:

> matrix(c(1,2,3,4,5,6), nrow = 2,
+            dimnames = list(gender = c("female","male"),
+                            politics = c("dem","ind","rep")))

        politics
gender   dem ind rep
  female   1   3   5
  male     2   4   6

    Technically speaking this is not a table object, it is a matrix, but it will suit our purposes.

15.2.2  From a data frame with xtabs

Most of the datasets from this chapter’s exercises look like this after importing:

> head(Dataset)

          School      Rating Frequency
1 Most desirable Outstanding        21
2           Good Outstanding         3
3       Adequate Outstanding        14
4    Undesirable Outstanding        10
5 Most desirable     Average        20
6           Good     Average        25

   The important thing about this is that there are two columns which represent the categorical variables,
and then a third column Frequency which gives the counts for each row/column combination. We make a
two-way table from such a data frame like this:

> xtabs(Frequency ~ School + Rating, data = Dataset)

                Rating
School           Average Outstanding Poor
  Adequate             8          14    2
  Good                25           3   36
  Most desirable      20          21    4
  Undesirable          7          10    6

   Sometimes, though, the data frame does not have a Frequency column, and instead it has a bunch of
repeated rows, one row for each observation falling in that row/column combination of the table, like this:

> head(A)

  gender politics
1 female      dem
2 female      dem
3 female      dem
4 female      dem
5 female      dem
6 female      dem



