15.3.2  Fisher’s Exact Test

The lady in question (Dr. Muriel Bristol) claimed to be able to tell whether the tea or the milk was added ﬁrst
to a cup. Fisher proposed to give her eight cups, four of each variety, in random order. This test calculates
the probability of her getting the speciﬁc number of cups she correctly identiﬁed by chance alone.

> LadyTastingTea = matrix(c(4,0,0,4), nrow = 2,
+                 dimnames = list(pouredFirst = c("milk","tea"),
+                 guessedFirst = c("milk","tea")))
> LadyTastingTea

           guessedFirst
pouredFirst milk tea
       milk    4   0
       tea     0   4

> fisher.test(LadyTastingTea, alternative = "greater")

        Fisher''s Exact Test for Count Data

data:  LadyTastingTea
p-value = 0.01429
alternative hypothesis: true odds ratio is greater than 1
95 percent confidence interval:
 2.003768      Inf
sample estimates:
odds ratio
       Inf

   These data provide evidence that Dr. Bristol really could tell which was added ﬁrst.

15.3.3  McNemar Test for Matched Pairs

For this type of problem we have matched pairs of data, for example, we may ask a single respondent on the
same survey twice, one before and one after a treatment of some kind. The hypotheses of interest are

                           H0 : π1+ = π+1 versus Ha : π1+ , π+1                              (15.7)

which after some algebra is equivalent to the hypotheses

                           H0 : π12 = π21 versus Ha : π12 , π21.                              (15.8)

   The test statistic is
                                                    n12 −n21
                                         χ2 =                ,                                        (15.9)
                                                    n12 + n21
and when the null hypothesis is true the statistic has a chi-square distribution with one degree of freedom.
There is a continuity correction proposed by Edwards1 that looks like this:

                                                        (|n12 −n21| −1)2
                                      χ2 =                             ,                                  (15.10)
                                                    n12 + n21

   The following example data from Agresti were about a panel of 1600 voting-age British citizens. They
were given a survey about whether they approved or disapproved of the Prime Minister’s performance, and
then those same 1600 people were asked again six months later.

   1Edwards, A (1948). “Note on the correction for continuity in testing the signiﬁcance of the diﬀerence between correlated propor-
tions”. Psychometrika. 13:185–187. doi:10.1007/bf02289261



15.4. CHI-SQUARE TESTS                                                            319


