15.4  Chi-square Tests

All chi-square tests are based on a test statistic χ2 which takes the general form

               X (Oi −Ei)2                                       χ2 =                        ,                                   (15.11)
                                                                                                        i      Ei

where Oi is an observed count (an entry in the table) and Ei is an expected value, that is, the value we
would expect if the null hypothesis is true. When H0 is true the test statistic has an (asymptotic) chi-square
distribution with some degrees of freedom (which depends on the speciﬁc hypotheses being tested).

15.4.1  Chi-square goodness of ﬁt

For this class of problems we have a univariate categorical variable (like gender or politics) and a hy-
pothesized model for the probabilities of the diﬀerent categories. We would like to see how well the model
ﬁts the data.
   Suppose the variable X has k categories, and we will write our hypothesized model this way:

                          P(X = category i) = πi, for i = 1, 2, . . . , k.                         (15.12)

We observe a random sample of size n. Here Oi is the number of observations in category i, so that O1 +O2 +
· · · + Ok = n. If the model truly holds then we would expect Ei = nπi observations in category i. The test
statistic is
                                                                  k
               X (Oi −Ei)2                                       χ2 =                                                  (15.13)
                                                        Ei                                                             i=1
and when H0 is true, the test statistic has a chi-square distribution with k −1 degrees of freedom.
   To do the test with R we enter the observed counts in a vector observed (could also be a one-way table)
and the hypothesized probabilities in a vector probs:



320                               CHAPTER 15. CATEGORICAL DATA ANALYSIS


> observed = c(160, 20, 10, 10)
> probs = c(0.50, 0.25, 0.10, 0.15)

   Then perform the test this way:

> chisq.test(observed, p = probs)

        Chi-squared test for given probabilities

data:  observed
X-squared = 72.333, df = 3, p-value = 1.351e-15

    In this case we reject the null hypothesis and the theoretical model does not ﬁt these data very well (at
all).


15.4.2  Chi-square tests of Independence and Homogeneity

The model is

                            Response 1  Response 2       Response c  Total
                  Group 1       π11           π12           · · ·       π1c       π1+
                  Group 2       π21           π22           · · ·       π2c       π2+
                                                                                                                               ...                                                        ...                                                                ...          ...                                       ...                                                ...                 (15.14)
                  Group r       πr1           πr2           · · ·        πrc       πr+
                        Total       π+1          π+2           · · ·      π+c       1

  We observe ni j observations from Group i with Response  j, with n being the total sample size, like this:

                            Response 1  Response 2       Response c  Total
                  Group 1       n11           n12           · · ·       n1c       n1+
                  Group 2       n21           n22           · · ·       n2c       n2+
                                                                                                                               ...                                                        ...                                                                ...          ...                                       ...                                                ...                 (15.15)
                  Group r       nr1            nr2           · · ·        nrc        nr+
                        Total        n+1          n+2           · · ·       n+c       n

   Here, the test statistic takes the general form

                            rX Xc  (Oi j −Ei j)2                                     χ2 =                                ,                                 (15.16)
                                                         Ei j                                                         i=1  j=1

but the hypotheses are diﬀerent so the expected counts are calculated diﬀerently and the degrees of freedom
are now (r −1)(c −1) where r is the number of rows and c is the number of columns.
   The conditions to use this test are

   • no Ei j is less than one, and

   • no more than 20% of the Eij’s are less than ﬁve.

If expected counts are too small then we can

   • combine categories provided there is a natural way to combine them, or

   • use exact tests



15.4. CHI-SQUARE TESTS                                                            321


Independence

Here we observe a single random sample and we place observations in the table depending on their classiﬁ-
cation status. The hypotheses of interest are

                         H0 : πij = πi+π+ j versus Ha : πi j , πi+π+ j,                         (15.17)

for i = 1, . . . , r and j = 1, . . . , c. The expected counts are

                                                 n+ j   ni+n+ j                                       Eij = nˆπi+ˆπ+ j = nni+   =            .                            (15.18)
                                            n  n      n

Homogeneity

Here we have a collection of r subpopulations and we observe simple random samples from each subpopula-
tion (it does not matter whether rows or columns represent the diﬀerent subpopulations). The hypotheses of
interest to us now are are

                   H0 : the row distributions (πi1, πi2, . . . πic) are all the same,                 (15.19)

for i = 1, . . . , r, versus
                      Ha : at least one row is diﬀerent from the others.                      (15.20)

The expected counts are calculated with the same formula, and the test is conducted the same way, but the
interpretation is diﬀerent.


How to do the tests

We have our data from above

> x = xtabs(Freq ~ Sex + Eye, data = HairEyeColor)
> x

        Eye
Sex      Brown Blue Hazel Green
  Male      98  101    47    33
  Female   122  114    46    31

  We can compare the row distributions with the following sequence of steps.

> addmargins(x, margin = 2)

        Eye
Sex      Brown Blue Hazel Green Sum
  Male      98  101    47    33 279
  Female   122  114    46    31 313

> prop.table(x, margin = 1)

        Eye
Sex           Brown       Blue      Hazel      Green
  Male   0.35125448 0.36200717 0.16845878 0.11827957
  Female 0.38977636 0.36421725 0.14696486 0.09904153

   The row distributions look similar to each other. Now we compare the column distributions.

