                           Sex: Male
                  1364             367

          No                              Yes
                                              Survived:                                                                                           Survived:


                  126              344
                          Sex: Female


                                     Figure 15.5: A fourfold plot.



Then the sample odds ratio (we usually just call it the odds ratio) is

                                              n11/n12    n11n22
                                                       ˆψ =      =            .                                  (15.25)
                                              n21/n22    n21n12

  We can compute the odds ratio by hand, or use the oddsratio function in the vcd package. Here is an
example table:


> x = xtabs(Freq ~ Sex + Survived, data = Titanic)
> x

        Survived
Sex        No  Yes
  Male   1364  367
  Female  126  344

> library(vcd)
> oddsratio(x, log = FALSE)

 odds ratios for Sex and Survived

[1] 10.14697


This means that the odds in favor of death were over 10 times higher for males than the odds for females (for
the Titanic disaster). We can make a visual display with a fourfold plot.


> fourfold(x)



15.6. COHRAN-MANTEL-HAENSZEL (CMH) TEST STATISTIC                            325

15.5.3  Asymptotic distribution of ln(ˆψ)

The distribution of ln( ˆψ) is asymptotically normal.

                         r 1    1    1    1                         ln(ˆψ) ·∼norm     = ln(ψ), sd =     +   +   +                     (15.26)                                                           mean                  n11   n12   n21   n22 ,

for n suﬃciently large.
   This information can be used to construct a large sample conﬁdence interval for ψ.

> confint(oddsratio(x, log = FALSE), level = 0.95)

                      2.5 %   97.5 %
Male:Female/No:Yes 8.026797 12.82715

15.6  Cohran-Mantel-Haenszel (CMH) Test Statistic

For this class of problems we have a binary predictor X (like treatment/placebo) and a categorical response
Y (like success/failure) but we also have another categorical variable Z (like democrat, republican, indepen-
dent. . . ) against which we would like to control. We assume that the odds ratios between X and Y are the
same at each level of Z, and we would like to know whether that common odds ratio ψ , 1.
   The CMH test statistic is
                                 hP k  n11k −n1+kn+1kn++k   i2
                    CMH =          n1+kn2+kn+1kn+2k     .                              (15.27)
                         P k   n2++k(n++k−1)
   The test statistic has an asymptotic chi-square distribution with one degree of freedom when the common
odds ratio ψ = 1.
  MH (1959) proposed a continuity correction in their original paper, and if used, the p-value better ap-
proximates an exact conditional test, but it tends to be conservative (Agresti).
   Note that if there is evidence that the odds ratio is diﬀerent for the diﬀerent levels of Z, then the CMH
test is not appropriate. We can check for evidence of this with a fourfold plot and/or the woolf_test, both
from package vcd.
   The following data are from the Titanic tragedy.

> x = xtabs(Freq ~ Sex + Age + Class, data = Titanic)
> ftable(x)

             Class 1st 2nd 3rd Crew
Sex    Age
Male   Child         5  11  48    0
       Adult       175 168 462  862
Female Child         1  13  31    0
       Adult       144  93 165   23

We check for similarity of odds ratios. Here are the diﬀerent odds ratios:

> oddsratio(x, log = FALSE)

 odds ratios for Sex and Age by Class

       1st        2nd        3rd       Crew
3.01899335 0.47268931 0.55095667 0.02724638

The estimates vary, but let us look to see if that variation is signiﬁcant.



