Appendix E

Mathematical Machinery




This appendix houses many of the standard deﬁnitions and theorems that are used at some point during the
narrative.  It is targeted for someone reading the book who forgets the precise deﬁnition of something and
would like a quick reminder of an exact statement. No proofs are given, and the interested reader should
consult a good text on Calculus (say, Stewart [194] or Apostol [6], [7]), Linear Algebra (say, Strang [196]
and Magnus [135]), Real Analysis (say, Folland [60], or Carothers [29]), or Measure Theory (Billingsley
[19], Ash [8], Resnick [173]) for details.


E.1  Set Algebra

We denote sets by capital letters, A, B, C, etc. The letter S is reserved for the sample space, also known as
the universe or universal set, the set which contains all possible elements. The symbol ∅represents the empty
set, the set with no elements.


E.1.1  Set Union, Intersection, and Diﬀerence

Given subsets A and B, we may manipulate them in an algebraic fashion. To this end, we have three set
operations at our disposal: union, intersection, and diﬀerence. Below is a table summarizing the pertinent
information about these operations.


E.1.2  Identities and Properties

   1. A ∪∅= A,  A ∩∅= ∅

   2. A ∪S = S,  A ∩S = A

   3. A ∪Ac = S , A ∩Ac = ∅

   4. (Ac)c = A


             Name        Denoted  Deﬁned by elements  R syntax
               Union       A ∪B    in A or B or both     union(A, B)
                   Intersection    A ∩B    in both A and B      intersect(A, B)
                Diﬀerence      A__OPEN_BOOK_BACKSLASH__B     in A but not in B     setdiff(A, B)
               Complement     Ac      in S but not in A     setdiff(S, A)

                                      Table E.1: Set operations


                                          347



348                                  APPENDIX E. MATHEMATICAL MACHINERY


   5. The Commutative Property:
                           A ∪B = B ∪A,  A ∩B = B ∩A                             (E.1)


   6. The Associative Property:

                       (A ∪B) ∪C = A ∪(B ∪C),  (A ∩B) ∩C = A ∩(B ∩C)                 (E.2)


   7. The Distributive Property:

               A ∪(B ∩C) = (A ∪B) ∩(A ∪C),  A ∩(B ∪C) = (A ∩B) ∪(A ∩C)           (E.3)


   8. DeMorgan’s Laws
                            (A ∪B)c = Ac ∩Bc  and  (A ∩B)c = Ac ∪Bc,                      (E.4)

      or more generally,
