E.2  Diﬀerential and Integral Calculus

A function  f of one variable is said to be one-to-one if no two distinct x values are mapped to the same
y = f(x) value. To show that a function is one-to-one we can either use the horizontal line test or we may
start with the equation f(x1) = f(x2) and use algebra to show that it implies x1 = x2.


E.2.1  Limits and Continuity

Deﬁnition E.1. Let f be a function deﬁned on some open interval that contains the number a, except possibly
at a itself. Then we say the limit of f(x) as x approaches a is L, and we write

                                           lim f(x) = L,                                          (E.6)
                                           x→a

if for every ϵ > 0 there exists a number δ > 0 such that 0 < |x −a| < δ implies | f(x) −L| < ϵ.

Deﬁnition E.2. A function f is continuous at a number a if

                                          lim f(x) = f(a).                                        (E.7)
                                          x→a

The function  f  is right-continuous at the number a if limx→a+ f(x) =  f(a), and left-continuous at a if
limx→a−f(x) = f(a). Finally, the function f is continuous on an interval I if it is continuous at every number
in the interval.


E.2.2  Diﬀerentiation

Deﬁnition E.3. The derivative of a function f at a number a, denoted by f ′(a), is

                                                  f(a + h) −f(a)
                                                          f ′(a) = lim                          ,                                  (E.8)
                                          h→0      h

provided this limit exists. A function is diﬀerentiable at a if  f ′(a) exists.  It is diﬀerentiable on an open
interval (a, b) if it is diﬀerentiable at every number in the interval.



E.2. DIFFERENTIAL AND INTEGRAL CALCULUS                                       349


Diﬀerentiation Rules

In the table that follows, f and g are diﬀerentiable functions and c is a constant.


                              dxcd  = 0            dxd xn = nxn−1       (cf)′ = cf ′

                                                                ′                                                             f  ′        f ′g−fg′
                                ( f ± g)′ =  f ± g′    ( fg)′ =  f ′g + fg′     g  =    g2


