E.3  Sequences and Series

A sequence is an ordered list of numbers, a1, a2, a3, . . . , an = (ak)nk=1. A sequence may be ﬁnite or inﬁnite.
In the latter case we write a1, a2, a3, . . . = (ak)∞k=1. We say that the inﬁnite sequence (ak)∞k=1 converges to the
ﬁnite limit L, and we write
                                      k→∞aklim  = L,                                        (E.17)

if for every ϵ > 0 there exists an integer N ≥1 such that |ak −L| < ϵ for all k ≥N. We say that the inﬁnite
sequence (ak)∞k=1 diverges to +∞(or −∞) if for every M ≥0 there exists an integer N ≥1 such that ak ≥M
for all k ≥N (or ak ≤−M for all k ≥N).


