E.6.2  Optimization

An function f of two variables has a local maximum at (a, b) if f(x, y) ≥f(a, b) for all points (x, y) near (a, b),
that is, for all points in an open disk centered at (a, b). The number f(a, b) is then called a local maximum
value of f. The function f has a local minimum if the same thing happens with the inequality reversed.
   Suppose the point (a, b) is a critical point of f, that is, suppose (a, b) satisﬁes

