        To specify the location of a point in space, we need three coordinates (x, y, z), where coordinates x and y specify
          locations in a plane, and coordinate z gives a vertical position above or below the plane. Three-dimensional space
        has three orthogonal directions, so we need not two but three unit vectors to define a three-dimensional coordinate
         system. In the Cartesian coordinate system, the first two unit vectors are the unit vector of the x-axis  and the unit
         vector of the y-axis   . The third unit vector   is the direction of the z-axis (Figure 2.21). The order in which the axes
         are labeled, which is the order in which the three unit vectors appear, is important because it defines the orientation




