                                                                        3.6 • Finding Velocity and Displacement from Acceleration       135


Significance
The acceleration function is linear in time so the integration involves simple polynomials. In Figure 3.30, we see that
if we extend the solution beyond the point when the velocity is zero, the velocity becomes negative and the boat
reverses direction. This tells us that solutions can give us information outside our immediate interest and we should
be careful when interpreting them.


