          Since            on Earth, the weight of a 1.00-kg object on Earth is 9.80 N:


        When the net external force on an object is its weight, we say that it is in free fall, that is, the only force acting on the
          object is gravity. However, when objects on Earth fall downward through the air, they are never truly in free fall
         because there is always some upward resistance force from the air acting on the object.

          Acceleration due to gravity g varies slightly over the surface of Earth, so the weight of an object depends on its
           location and is not an intrinsic property of the object. Weight varies dramatically if we leave Earth’s surface. On the



