         There are two important features of Newton’s third law. First, the forces exerted (the action and reaction) are always
          equal in magnitude but opposite in direction. Second, these forces are acting on different bodies or systems: A’s
           force acts on B and B’s force acts on A. In other words, the two forces are distinct forces that do not act on the same
          body. Thus, they do not cancel each other.

