In Newton’s Laws of Motion, we discussed the normal force, which is a contact force that acts normal to the surface
so that an object does not have an acceleration perpendicular to the surface. The bathroom scale is an excellent
example of a normal force acting on a body. It provides a quantitative reading of how much it must push upward to
support the weight of an object. But can you predict what you would see on the dial of a bathroom scale if you stood
on it during an elevator ride? Will you see a value greater than your weight when the elevator starts up? What about
when the elevator moves upward at a constant speed? Take a guess before reading the next example.

