One of the real triumphs of Newton’s law of universal gravitation, with the force proportional to the inverse of the
distance squared, is that when it is combined with his second law, the solution for the path of any satellite is a conic
section. Every path taken by m is one of the four conic sections: a circle or an ellipse for bound or closed orbits, or a
parabola or hyperbola for unbounded or open orbits. These conic sections are shown in Figure 13.18.
