at more than 3000 locations around the United States.


The magnitude of the tides, however, is far more complicated. The relative angles of Earth and the Moon determine
spring and neap tides, but the magnitudes of these tides are affected by the distances from Earth as well. Tidal
forces are greater when the distances are smaller. Both the Moon’s orbit about Earth and Earth’s orbit about the Sun
are elliptical, so a spring tide is exceptionally large if it occurs when the Moon is at perigee and Earth is at perihelion.
Conversely, it is relatively small if it occurs when the Moon is at apogee and Earth is at aphelion.

The greatest causes of tide variation are the topography of the local shoreline and the bathymetry (the profile of the
depth) of the ocean floor. The range of tides due to these effects is astounding. Although ocean tides are much
smaller than a meter in many places around the globe, the tides at the Bay of Fundy (Figure 13.25), on the east
coast of Canada, can be as much as 16.3 meters.





