Hydraulic systems can increase or decrease the force applied to them. To make the force larger, the pressure is
applied to a larger area. For example, if a 100-N force is applied to the left cylinder in Figure 14.16 and the right
cylinder has an area five times greater, then the output force is 500 N. Hydraulic systems are analogous to simple
levers, but they have the advantage that pressure can be sent through tortuously curved lines to several places at
once.

The hydraulic jack is such a hydraulic system. A hydraulic jack is used to lift heavy loads, such as the ones used by
auto mechanics to raise an automobile. It consists of an incompressible fluid in a U-tube fitted with a movable
piston on each side. One side of the U-tube is narrower than the other. A small force applied over a small area can
balance a much larger force on the other side over a larger area (Figure 14.17).
