Numerous lower-density objects or substances float in higher-density fluids: oil on water, a hot-air balloon in the
atmosphere, a bit of cork in wine, an iceberg in salt water, and hot wax in a “lava lamp,” to name a few. A less
obvious example is mountain ranges floating on the higher-density crust and mantle beneath them. Even seemingly
solid Earth has fluid characteristics.
