Figure 15.31 shows a graph of the amplitude of a damped harmonic oscillator as a function of the frequency of the
periodic force driving it. Each of the three curves on the graph represents a different amount of damping. All three
curves peak at the point where the frequency of the driving force equals the natural frequency of the harmonic
oscillator. The highest peak, or greatest response, is for the least amount of damping, because less energy is
removed by the damping force. Note that since the amplitude grows as the damping decreases, taking this to the
