         The time-averaged power of a sinusoidal mechanical wave, which is the average rate of energy transfer associated
          with a wave as it passes a point, can be found by taking the total energy associated with the wave divided by the
          time it takes to transfer the energy. If the velocity of the sinusoidal wave is constant, the time for one wavelength to
         pass by a point is equal to the period of the wave, which is also constant. For a sinusoidal mechanical wave, the
          time-averaged power is therefore the energy associated with a wavelength divided by the period of the wave. The
         wavelength of the wave divided by the period is equal to the velocity of the wave,

                                                                                                     16.10


         Note that this equation for the time-averaged power of a sinusoidal mechanical wave shows that the power is
          proportional to the square of the amplitude of the wave and to the square of the angular frequency of the wave.
           Recall that the angular frequency is equal to                , so the power of a mechanical wave is equal to the square of
          the amplitude and the square of the frequency of the wave.

