         such “Doppler Radar” can give the velocity and direction of rain or snow in weather fronts. In astronomy, we can
         examine the light emitted from distant galaxies and determine their speed relative to ours. As galaxies move away
         from us, their light is shifted to a lower frequency, and so to a longer wavelength—the so-called red shift. Such
          information from galaxies far, far away has allowed us to estimate the age of the universe (from the Big Bang) as
         about 14 × 1010 years.
