The first law is a statement of energy conservation. It tells us that a system can exchange energy with its
surroundings by the transmission of heat and by the performance of work. The net energy exchanged is then equal
to the change in the total mechanical energy of the molecules of the system (i.e., the system’s internal energy).
Thus, if a system is isolated, its internal energy must remain constant.

Although Q and W both depend on the thermodynamic path taken between two equilibrium states, their difference
