Carnot summarized his study of the Carnot engine and Carnot cycle into what is now known as Carnot’s principle:

 CARNOT’S PRINCIPLE

 No engine working between two reservoirs at constant temperatures can have a greater efficiency than a
  reversible engine.


This principle can be viewed as another statement of the second law of thermodynamics and can be shown to be
equivalent to the Kelvin statement and the Clausius statement.

