Significance
The results show that as the radial distance increases inside the thick wire, the magnetic field increases from zero to
a familiar value of the magnetic field of a thin wire. Outside the wire, the field drops off regardless of whether it was
a thick or thin wire.

This result is similar to how Gauss’s law for electrical charges behaves inside a uniform charge distribution, except
that Gauss’s law for electrical charges has a uniform volume distribution of charge, whereas Ampère’s law here has
a uniform area of current distribution. Also, the drop-off outside the thick wire is similar to how an electric field
drops off outside of a linear charge distribution, since the two cases have the same geometry and neither case
depends on the configuration of charges or currents once the loop is outside the distribution.


