INTRODUCTION In Electromagnetic Induction, we discussed how a time-varying magnetic flux induces an emf in a
circuit. In many of our calculations, this flux was due to an applied time-dependent magnetic field. The reverse of
this phenomenon also occurs: The current flowing in a circuit produces its own magnetic field.

In Electromagnetic Induction, we also saw that induction is the process by which an emf is induced by changing
electric flux and separation of a dipole. So far, we have discussed some examples of induction, although some of
