The Ray Model of Light

You have already studied some of the wave characteristics of light in the previous chapter on Electromagnetic
Waves (http://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/16-introduction). In this chapter, we start
mainly with the ray characteristics. There are three ways in which light can travel from a source to another location
(Figure 1.4). It can come directly from the source through empty space, such as from the Sun to Earth. Or light can
travel through various media, such as air and glass, to the observer. Light can also arrive after being reflected, such
as by a mirror. In all of these cases, we can model the path of light as a straight line called a ray.





FIGURE 1.4 Three methods for light to travel from a source to another location. (a) Light reaches the upper atmosphere of Earth, traveling
through empty space directly from the source. (b) Light can reach a person by traveling through media like air and glass. (c) Light can also
Access for free at openstax.org.
