        As noted, the image must fall precisely on the retina to produce clear vision—that is, the image distance   must
         equal the lens-to-retina distance. Because the lens-to-retina distance does not change, the image distance   must
        be the same for objects at all distances. The ciliary muscles adjust the shape of the eye lens for focusing on nearby
         or far objects. By changing the shape of the eye lens, the eye changes the focal length of the lens. This mechanism
          of the eye is called accommodation.

        The nearest point an object can be placed so that the eye can form a clear image on the retina is called the near
         point of the eye. Similarly, the far point is the farthest distance at which an object is clearly visible. A person with
        normal vision can see objects clearly at distances ranging from 25 cm to essentially infinity. The near point increases
         with age, becoming several meters for some older people. In this text, we consider the near point to be 25 cm.

