diverging lens must form an image of distant (physical) objects at a point that is closer than the far point.





FIGURE 2.32 Correction of nearsightedness requires a diverging lens that compensates for overconvergence by the eye. The diverging lens
produces an image closer to the eye than the physical object. This image serves as the optical object for the eye, and the nearsighted
person can see it clearly because it is closer than their far point.

