       Telescopes

         Telescopes are meant for viewing distant objects and produce an image that is larger than the image produced in the
        unaided eye. Telescopes gather far more light than the eye, allowing dim objects to be observed with greater
         magnification and better resolution. Telescopes were invented around 1600, and Galileo was the first to use them to
         study the heavens, with monumental consequences. He observed the moons of Jupiter, the craters and mountains
        on the moon, the details of sunspots, and the fact that the Milky Way is composed of a vast number of individual
          stars.





