        same effect, but the deciding parameter for light is the index of refraction. Light waves undergo a      or  radians
         phase change upon reflection at an interface beyond which is a medium of higher index of refraction. No phase
         change takes place when reflecting from a medium of lower refractive index (Figure 3.13). Because of the periodic
          nature of waves, this phase change or inversion is equivalent to       in distance travelled, or path length. Both the
          path length and refractive indices are important factors in thin-film interference.





