Diffraction gratings work both for transmission of light, as in Figure 4.14, and for reflection of light, as on butterfly
wings and the Australian opal in Figure 4.15. Natural diffraction gratings also occur in the feathers of certain birds
such as the hummingbird. Tiny, finger-like structures in regular patterns act as reflection gratings, producing
constructive interference that gives the feathers colors not solely due to their pigmentation. This is called
iridescence.





