       4.5 Circular Apertures and Resolution

        LEARNING OBJECTIVES
        By the end of this section, you will be able to:
                •  Describe the diffraction limit on resolution
                •  Describe the diffraction limit on beam propagation

           Light diffracts as it moves through space, bending around obstacles, interfering constructively and destructively.
          This can be used as a spectroscopic tool—a diffraction grating disperses light according to wavelength, for example,
         and is used to produce spectra—but diffraction also limits the detail we can obtain in images.

          Figure 4.17(a) shows the effect of passing light through a small circular aperture. Instead of a bright spot with sharp
          edges, we obtain a spot with a fuzzy edge surrounded by circles of light. This pattern is caused by diffraction, similar
           to that produced by a single slit. Light from different parts of the circular aperture interferes constructively and
           destructively. The effect is most noticeable when the aperture is small, but the effect is there for large apertures as
