            All attempts to observe the size and shape of objects are limited by the wavelength of the probe. Even the small
         wavelength of light prohibits exact precision. When extremely small wavelength probes are used, as with an
          electron microscope, the system is disturbed, still limiting our knowledge. Heisenberg’s uncertainty principle asserts
           that this limit is fundamental and inescapable, as we shall see in the chapter on quantum mechanics.

