The answer in part (b) indicates that two stars separated by about half a light-year can be resolved. The average
distance between stars in a galaxy is on the order of five light-years in the outer parts and about one light-year near
the galactic center. Therefore, the Hubble can resolve most of the individual stars in Andromeda Galaxy, even
though it lies at such a huge distance that its light takes 2 million years to reach us. Figure 4.20 shows another
mirror used to observe radio waves from outer space.





