spacing of atoms in various crystals was reasonably well known at the time, based on good values for Avogadro’s
number.) The experiments were convincing, and the 1914 Nobel Prize in Physics was given to von Laue for his
suggestion leading to the proof that X-rays are EM waves. In 1915, the unique father-and-son team of Sir William
Henry Bragg and his son Sir William Lawrence Bragg were awarded a joint Nobel Prize for inventing the X-ray
spectrometer and the then-new science of X-ray analysis.

