          Here, the relative velocity between observers affects whether two events a distance apart are observed to be
          simultaneous. Simultaneityisnotabsolute. We might have guessed (incorrectly) that if light is emitted
          simultaneously, then two observers halfway between the sources would see the flashes simultaneously. But careful
           analysis shows this cannot be the case if the speed of light is the same in all inertial frames.

          This type of thoughtexperiment (in German, “Gedankenexperiment”) shows that seemingly obvious conclusions
         must be changed to agree with the postulates of relativity. The validity of thought experiments can only be
         determined by actual observation, and careful experiments have repeatedly confirmed Einstein’s theory of relativity.





