       The Twin Paradox

        An intriguing consequence of time dilation is that a space traveler moving at a high velocity relative to Earth would
         age less than the astronaut’s earthbound twin. This is often known as the twin paradox. Imagine the astronaut
         moving at such a velocity that         as in Figure 5.7. A trip that takes 2.00 years in her frame would take 60.0
          years in the earthbound twin’s frame. Suppose the astronaut travels 1.00 year to another star system, briefly
          explores the area, and then travels 1.00 year back. An astronaut who was 40 years old at the start of the trip would
         be 42 when the spaceship returns. Everything on Earth, however, would have aged 60.0 years. The earthbound twin,
