       The Work Function

         The photoelectric effect was explained in 1905 by A. Einstein. Einstein reasoned that if Planck’s hypothesis about
          energy quanta was correct for describing the energy exchange between electromagnetic radiation and cavity walls,
                it should also work to describe energy absorption from electromagnetic radiation by the surface of a photoelectrode.
        He postulated that an electromagnetic wave carries its energy in discrete packets. Einstein’s postulate goes beyond
          Planck’s hypothesis because it states that the light itself consists of energy quanta. In other words, it states that
          electromagnetic waves are quantized.

          In Einstein’s approach, a beam of monochromatic light of frequency f is made of photons. A photon is a particle of
