In Einstein’s interpretation, interactions take place between individual electrons and individual photons. The
absence of a lag time means that these one-on-one interactions occur instantaneously. This interaction time cannot
be increased by lowering the light intensity. The light intensity corresponds to the number of photons arriving at the
metal surface per unit time. Even at very low light intensities, the photoelectric effect still occurs because the
interaction is between one electron and one photon. As long as there is at least one photon with enough energy to
transfer it to a bound electron, a photoelectron will appear on the surface of the photoelectrode.

