        Covalent Bonding in Solids

           Crystals can also be formed by covalent bonding. For example, covalent bonds are responsible for holding carbon
         atoms together in diamond crystals. The electron configuration of the carbon atom is        —a He core plus
           four valence electrons. This electron configuration is four electrons short of a full shell, so by sharing these four
          electrons with other carbon atoms in a covalent bond, the shells of all carbon atoms are filled. Diamond has a more
          complicated structure than most ionic crystals (Figure 9.11). Each carbon atom is the center of a regular
          tetrahedron, and the angle between the bonds is      This angle is a direct consequence of the directionality of the
      p orbitals of carbon atoms.





