       9.7 Semiconductor Devices

        LEARNING OBJECTIVES
        By the end of this section, you will be able to:
                •  Describe what occurs when n- and p-type materials are joined together using the concept of diffusion and
                    drift current (zero applied voltage)
                •  Explain the response of a p-n junction to a forward and reverse bias voltage
                •  Describe the function of a transistor in an electric circuit
                •  Use the concept of a p-n junction to explain its applications in audio amplifiers and computers

         Semiconductor technology has been a driver of the computing, information technology, mobile device, and related
           industries. The transistor — a device that can amplify or switch electrical signals — is the key component. In the late
        1940s and early 1950s, a number of discoveries and inventions demonstrated the promise of this technology.
          Researchers William Shockley, John Bardeen, and Walter Brattain invented the first working transistor, and followed
        up with several other advances. Soon after, their colleague Mohammad M. Atalla developed a surface process that
            significantly improved transistor performance, and enabled researchers (and eventually manufacturers) to utilize
            silicon as the primary material. With Dawon Kahng, Atalla went on to invent a specific type of transistor, the
        MOSFET, which could be miniaturized and therefore incorporated into a high-density integrated circuit (abbreviated
              ic). Chih-Tang Sah and Frank Wanlass built on this work by developing a MOSFET circuit fabrication method that
         would become (and still is) the dominant way to manufacture microprocessors.

         The results of these efforts were widespread and immediate. Smaller and more efficient computing devices led to
         advances in arenas ranging from spaceflight (where every gram counts) to wristwatches to radios. Computers
        became smaller and smaller, enabling businesses and everyday consumers to own them and eventually carry them
            in their pockets in the form of smartphones. These changes have all been driven by silicon semiconductors, leading
         a region known for electronics to be renamed Silicon Valley.

       We describe two basic semiconductor devices below.
       Diodes

         Perhaps the simplest device that can be created with a semiconductor is a diode. A diode is a circuit element that
          allows electric current to flow in only one direction, like a one-way valve (see Model of Conduction in Metals
          (http://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-2-model-of-conduction-in-metals)). A diode is
          created by joining a p-type semiconductor to an n-type semiconductor (Figure 9.23). The junction between these
          materials is called a p-n junction. A comparison of the energy bands of a silicon-based diode is shown in Figure
           9.23(b). The positions of the valence and conduction bands are the same, but the impurity levels are quite different.
        When a p-n junction is formed, electrons from the conduction band of the n-type material diffuse to the p-side,
         where they combine with holes in the valence band. This migration of charge leaves positive ionized donor ions on
          the n-side and negative ionized acceptor ions on the p-side, producing a narrow double layer of charge at the p-n
           junction called the depletion layer. The electric field associated with the depletion layer prevents further diffusion.




