         The behavior of a semiconductor diode can now be understood. If the positive side of the battery is connected to the
          n-type material, the depletion layer is widened, and the potential energy difference across the p-n junction is
          increased. Few or none of the electrons (holes) have enough energy to climb the potential barrier, and current is
            significantly reduced. This is called the reverse bias configuration. On the other hand, if the positive side of a
          battery is connected to the p-type material, the depletion layer is narrowed, the potential energy difference across
          the p-n junction is reduced, and electrons (holes) flow easily. This is called the forward bias configuration of the
          diode. In sum, the diode allows current to flow freely in one direction but prevents current flow in the opposite
           direction. In this sense, the semiconductor diode is a one-way valve.

