The forces that bind nucleons together in an atomic nucleus are much greater than those that bind an electron to an
atom through electrostatic attraction. This is evident by the relative sizes of the atomic nucleus and the atom
                       respectively). The energy required to pry a nucleon from the nucleus is therefore much larger
than that required to remove (or ionize) an electron in an atom. In general, all nuclear changes involve large
amounts of energy per particle undergoing the reaction. This has numerous practical applications.
