          Nuclear radiation damages cells by ionizing atoms in the cells as they pass through the cells (Figure 10.30). The
           effects of ionizing radiation depend on the dose in rads, but also on the type of radiation (alpha, beta, gamma, or X-
           ray) and the type of tissue. For example, if the range of the radiation is small, as it is for   rays, then the ionization
         and the damage created is more concentrated and harder for the organism to repair. To account for such affects, we
          define the relative biological effectiveness (RBE). Sample RBE values for several types of ionizing nuclear radiation
          are given in Table 10.3.





