    While some may argue that as a White filmmaker Hitchcock does not have a responsibility to reflect the Black
    experience accurately in his films, their argument falls apart with Hitchcock’s use of “audio blackface.”
    Hitchcock accepts the responsibility of including the Black perspective, but he fails in his presentation, and the
     film suffers for it when Hitchcock goes for a laugh at the expense of the Black community. Instead of elevating
    Black culture and experiences through his use of Black characters, he portrays their point of view as invalid and
    perpetuates their oppression. Hitchcock doesn’t just ignore the existence of Black people, he uses them to
    promote a racist agenda.

