    BlackPanther.

         Malian writer, filmmaker, and cultural theorist Manthia Diawara describes the “manner in which black
          spectators may . . . resist the persuasive elements of Hollywood narrative” as a “challenge to . . . spectatorship
        and the aesthetics of Afro-American independent cinema” (845). The 2018 movie BlackPanther may be
         understood as an answer to Diawara’s challenge. Skillfully directed by African American screenwriter Ryan
          Coogler, BlackPanther is an example of the aesthetic beauty and financial success that art can achieve when
         not only are Black characters cast but also when Black storylines are expressed by the people who have had
          firsthand experiences.

