    AnotherLens. To gain another analytic view on the topic of your report, consider different people affected by
        it. Say, for example, that you have decided to report on recent high school graduates and the effect of the
    COVID-19 pandemic on the final months of their senior year. If you are a recent high school graduate, you
     might naturally gravitate toward writing about yourself and your peers. But you might also consider the adults
     in the lives of recent high school graduates—for example, teachers, parents, or grandparents—and how they
     view the same period. Or you might consider the same topic from the perspective of a college admissions
     department looking at their incoming freshman class.

        Quick Launch: Finding and Focusing a Topic

    Coming up with a topic for a report can be daunting because you can report on nearly anything. The topic can
      easily get too broad, trapping you in the realm of generalizations. The trick is to find a topic that interests you
    and focus on an angle you can analyze in order to say something significant about it. You can use a graphic
     organizer to generate ideas, or you can use a concept map similar to the one featured in Writing Process:



