    Discussion Questions

     1. For what purpose might Johnson have chosen to address the American people under the guise of a
         graduation address?

     2. What parts of Johnson’s speech show that he is trying to connect with the students in the audience?

     3. For what reasons has Johnson singled out the cities, countrysides, and schools as the locations of his Great
         Society?

     4. Johnson acknowledges one main counterclaim to the ideas proposed in the Great Society. How does
        Johnson address that counterclaim?

     5. In today’s political climate, Johnson’s Great Society might be labeled by some as socialism, an economic
        system in which production, distribution, and exchange of goods are owned or governed by the
       community as a whole rather than by individuals. In what way might Johnson have responded to this
         counterclaim?

     6. Johnson ends with a reference to the founders of the country and says, “You can make their vision our
           reality.” In your opinion, does he adequately explain what he means by “our reality”? Why or why not?





