        The results show “it is unlikely that plant-based diets provide advantages, but do not suffer from
          disadvantages, compared to omnivorous diets for strength, anaerobic, or aerobic exercise performance” (1).

       A second objection addresses the claim that land use for animal-based food production contributes to pollution
        and greenhouse gas emissions and is inefficient in terms of nutrient delivery. Berners-Lee et al. point out that
         animal nutrition from grass, pasture, and silage comes partially from land that cannot be used for other
         purposes, such as producing food directly edible by humans or for other ecosystem services such as biofuel
          production. Consequently, nutritional losses from such land use do not fully translate into losses of human-
          available nutrients (3).

