    Formatting

    The format of an annotated bibliography can vary depending on the discipline and purpose. Therefore, be
     clear at the outset which style you need to use. In academic writing, that information often will come from your
      instructor. Generally, annotated bibliographies will be written in MLA Documentation and Format, APA
    Documentation and Format, or Chicago style.

     This text uses MLA style. MLA source citations follow several principles in place of specific rules. The style has
    been adapted in recent years to respond to the evolving nature of text in an increasingly digital world. Thus,
     the MLAHandbook is organized by the process of citation, rather than listing rules for every type of source.
     However, certain overall guidelines apply. When you cite a source, first identify core elements that are present.
    Remember from earlier in this chapter that these are author(s), title of source, title of container, version,
    number, publisher, publication date, and location.





