Introduction





   FIGURE 15.4 Pierre Paul Broca (credit: “Paul Broca” by Anonymous/Wikimedia Commons, Public Domain)

French physician Pierre Paul Broca (1824–1880), an early researcher of brain science, is credited with
developing theories about how the brain controls motor functions such as language. One of his patients, Louis
Victor Leborgne, suffered from a continuing loss of speech that resulted in an inability to form any syllables
other than tan. Following Leborgne’s death, Broca examined Leborgne’s brain and observed some
abnormalities in the left frontal lobe. These observations would eventually lead to the larger discovery that
different areas of the brain control different motor functions.

This sample case study imagines what Broca might have written about Leborgne. The contemporary
references at the end of the study are those used by the actual writer of the case study, not Pierre Paul Broca.
