    Lacking any physical irregularities that would cause his loss of speech, I conclude the condition must be related
     to brain function. This condition is so singular that it seems useful to assign it a special name. I will designate it,
     therefore, as aphemia, or loss of speech.

    The patient expired on 17 April 1861 at 11:00 a.m. Cause of death was gangrenous infection and sepsis.

