Only 2.2% of the population has an IQ score below 70 (American Psychiatric Association [APA], 2013). If a
person earns a score approximately two standard deviations below the mean on an intelligence test, (about 70
on a test with a mean of 100), has major deficits in adaptive functioning, and these cognitive and adaptive
deficits were present before the age of 18, they could be diagnosed as having an intellectual disability (ID).
Formerly known as mental retardation, the accepted term now is intellectual disability, and it has four
subtypes: mild, moderate, severe, and profound (Table 7.4). TheDiagnosticandStatisticalManualof
