    Many states do have usury laws, which impose an upper limit on the interest rate that lenders can charge.
     However, in many cases these upper limits are well above the market interest rate. For example, if the interest
     rate is not allowed to rise above 30% per year, it can still fluctuate below that level according to market forces.
