long-run average cost curve determines how many firms will exist in a given industry.

If the quantity demanded in the market of a certain product is much greater than the quantity found at the
bottom of the long-run average cost curve, where the cost of production is lowest, the market will have many
firms competing. If the quantity demanded in the market is less than the quantity at the bottom of the LRAC,
there will likely be only one firm.
