     impossible to charge listeners directly for listening to conventional radio broadcasts.

     Radio has found a way to collect revenue by selling advertising, which is an indirect way of “charging” listeners
    by taking up some of their time. Ultimately, consumers who purchase the goods advertised are also paying for
     the radio service, since the station builds in the cost of advertising into the product cost. In a more recent
     development, satellite radio companies, such as SiriusXM, charge a regular subscription fee for streaming
    music without commercials. In this case, however, the product is excludable—only those who pay for the
     subscription will receive the broadcast.

    Some public goods will also have a mixture of public provision at no charge along with fees for some purposes,
      like a public city park that is free to use, but the government charges a fee for parking your car, for reserving
     certain picnic grounds, and for food sold at a refreshment stand.

