        •  Analyze various arguments for and against economic convergence
        •  Evaluate the speed of economic convergence between high-income countries and the rest of the world

    Some low-income and middle-income economies around the world have shown a pattern of convergence, in
    which their economies grow faster than those of high-income countries. GDP increased by an average rate of
    2.7% per year in the 1990s and 1.7% per year from 2010 to 2019 in the high-income countries of the world,
    which include the United States, Canada, the European Union countries, Japan, Australia, and New Zealand.

