     However, we need to treat cross-country comparisons of unemployment rates with care, because each country
    has slightly different definitions of unemployment, survey tools for measuring unemployment, and also
     different labor markets. For example, Japan’s unemployment rates appear quite low, but Japan’s economy has
    been mired in slow growth and recession since the late 1980s, and Japan’s unemployment rate probably paints
     too rosy a picture of its labor market. In Japan, workers who lose their jobs are often quick to exit the labor
     force and not look for a new job, in which case they are not counted as unemployed. In addition, Japanese
     firms are often quite reluctant to fire workers, and so firms have substantial numbers of workers who are on
    reduced hours or officially employed, but doing very little. We can view this Japanese pattern as an unusual



