       BRING IT HOME

    Unemployment and the COVID-19 Pandemic
     Almost two years after the pandemic began, the unemployment rate was on track to go below 4% again by early- to
     mid-2022. While this is great news for workers, we have also seen that the story of the labor market is more
     complicated than a single statistic might suggest. Millions remained out of the labor force due to the public health
      situation, and the percentage of workers unemployed for longer than 26 weeks was still high.

    The shift to remote work helped the unemployment rate come down by providing greater flexibility for workers
     concerned with their health and safety. But it didn''t help workers, especially women, who continued to be burdened
     with excessive care responsibilities at home. During 2020, the unemployment rate for women exceeded that of men
     by over a full percentage point. Additionally, virus variants in other countries threatened to disrupt progress made at
    home and abroad.

    The pandemic has helped shed light on how we can use information from all areas of the labor market to evaluate
     the health of the economy.





