In the last three decades, inflation has been relatively low in the U.S. economy, with the Consumer Price Index
typically rising 2% to 4% per year. Looking back over the twentieth century, there have been several periods
where inflation caused the price level to rise at double-digit rates, but nothing has come close to
hyperinflation.

