    As economies grew and became more global in nature, the use of commodity monies became more
    cumbersome. Countries moved towards the use of fiat money. Fiat money has no intrinsic value, but is
     declared by a government to be a country''s legal tender. The United States’ paper money, for example, carries
     the statement: “THIS NOTE IS LEGAL TENDER FOR ALL DEBTS, PUBLIC AND PRIVATE.” In other words, by
    government decree, if you owe a debt, then legally speaking, you can pay that debt with the U.S. currency, even
    though it is not backed by a commodity. The only backing of our money is universal faith and trust that the
     currency has value, and nothing more.

