Is a Stronger Dollar Good for the U.S. Economy?
From 2002 to 2008, the U.S. dollar lost more than a quarter of its value in foreign currency markets. On January 1,
2002, one dollar was worth 1.11 euros. On April 24, 2008 it hit its lowest point with a dollar being worth 0.64 euros.
During this period, the trade deficit between the United States and the European Union grew from a yearly total of
approximately 85.7 billion dollars in 2002 to 95.8 billion dollars in 2008. Was this a good thing or a bad thing for the
U.S. economy?

We live in a global world. U.S. consumers buy trillions of dollars worth of imported goods and services each year, not
just from the European Union, but from all over the world. U.S. businesses sell trillions of dollars’ worth of exports.
U.S. citizens, businesses, and governments invest trillions of dollars abroad every year. Foreign investors,
