       CLEAR IT UP

   Why is a stronger currency not necessarily better?
    One common misunderstanding about exchange rates is that a “stronger” or “appreciating” currency must be better
     than a “weaker” or “depreciating” currency. After all, is it not obvious that “strong” is better than “weak”? Do not let
     the terminology confuse you. When a currency becomes stronger, so that it purchases more of other currencies, it
     benefits some in the economy and injures others. Stronger currency is not necessarily better, it is just different.


