     rate stood at 103 yen per dollar. As investor sentiment swings back and forth, driving exchange rates up and
    down, exporters, importers, and banks involved in international lending are all affected. At worst, large
    movements in exchange rates can drive companies into bankruptcy or trigger a nationwide banking collapse.
     However, even in the moderate case of the yen/dollar exchange rate, these movements of roughly 30 percent
    back and forth impose stress on both economies as firms must alter their export and import plans to take the
    new exchange rates into account. Especially in smaller countries where international trade is a relatively large
     share of GDP, exchange rate movements can rattle their economies.





    FIGURE 29.11 U.S. Dollar Exchange Rate in Japanese Yen Even seemingly stable exchange rates such as the
     Japanese Yen to the U.S. Dollar can vary when closely examined over time. This figure shows a relatively stable rate
    between 2000 and 2007 of over 25%. Beginning in 2007 until 2012, there was a significant appreciation of the Yen
      (relative to the U.S. Dollar) by about 14% and again at the end of the year in 2014 also by about 14%. Since then,
