Low-income countries are at a disadvantage because any incomes that people receive are spent immediately
on necessities such as food. People in these countries live on less than $1,035 per year, which is less than $100
per month. Lack of saving means a lack of capital accumulation and a lack of loanable funds for investment in
physical and human capital. Recent research by two MIT economists, Abhijit Bannerjee and Esther Duflo, has
confirmed that the households in these economies are trapped in low incomes because they cannot muster
enough investment to push themselves out of poverty.

For example, the average citizen of Burundi, a low-income country, subsists on $239 per year (adjusted to
2020 dollars). According to Central Intelligence Agency data in its CIA Factbook, as of 2021, 85% of Burundi’s
population is agrarian, with bananas as the main income producing crop. Only one in two children attends
school and, as Figure 32.6 shows, many are not in schools comparable to what occurs in developed countries.
Political instability has made it difficult for Burundi to make significant headway toward growth, as verified by
the electrification of only 11% of households and 40% of its national income coming from foreign aid.





