of all final output that is produced and sold must be income to someone, somewhere in the economy. At a national
income level of zero, $600 is consumed. Then, each time income rises by $1,000, consumption rises by $800,
because in this example, the marginal propensity to consume is 0.8.

The pattern of consumption shown in Table D1 is plotted in Figure D2. To calculate consumption, multiply the
income level by 0.8, for the marginal propensity to consume, and add $600, for the amount that would be
